WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.560
Tyler: Welcome everyone.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.000
Tyler: I'm Tyler Yahn.

3
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.720
Nikola: And I'm Nikola Grcevski.

4
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.531
Tyler: Imagine you've clicked Place Order.

5
00:00:07.531 --> 00:00:08.426
Tyler: Now you're waiting.

6
00:00:08.426 --> 00:00:09.321
Tyler: Did it work?

7
00:00:09.321 --> 00:00:10.514
Tyler: Should you click again?

8
00:00:10.514 --> 00:00:13.795
Tyler: Our checkout succeeds, but the customer waits more than two seconds.

9
00:00:13.795 --> 00:00:16.480
Tyler: We're going to find that wait and remove it.

10
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.438
Nikola: Then we'll make the same diagnostic detail available to the other teams, without

11
00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:23.936
Nikola: repeating the agent setup for every service.

12
00:00:23.936 --> 00:00:25.720
Nikola: First, let's follow the order.

13
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.689
Tyler: The request enters through Gateway, reaches Checkout and Inventory, then fans

14
00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:32.343
Tyler: out through the commerce services.

15
00:00:32.343 --> 00:00:34.328
Tyler: Eighteen applications, plus PostgreSQL and Redis.

16
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:39.290
Tyler: We know the route; we don't yet know which part is making the customer wait.

17
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.803
Nikola: And the customer shouldn't need to know.

18
00:00:41.803 --> 00:00:45.250
Nikola: Let's see the request before we choose a service to investigate.

19
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.368
Tyler: We'll start with OpenTelemetry eBPF Instrumentation, or OBI.

20
00:00:49.368 --> 00:00:53.915
Tyler: Deploy it on the Linux nodes and it generates OpenTelemetry traces and metrics

21
00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:54.965
Tyler: for supported applications.

22
00:00:54.965 --> 00:00:57.763
Tyler: No application code changes and no application restarts.

23
00:00:57.763 --> 00:01:02.310
Tyler: That's a pretty good way to meet eighteen services you haven't instrumented yet.

24
00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:06.532
Nikola: If you joined us in Atlanta last year, you'll recognize the idea.

25
00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:09.133
Nikola: OBI is now version one and generally available.

26
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.083
Nikola: The previous talk is linked here.

27
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:15.310
Nikola: Today, we're using that broad view as the starting point for an investigation.

28
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.553
Tyler: Let's deploy OBI and send an order.

29
00:01:17.553 --> 00:01:19.750
Tyler: The application keeps running while we add visibility.

30
00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.820
Nikola: One deployment on the nodes.

31
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.550
Nikola: Let's open the request we just sent.

32
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.872
Nikola: Here's that order in Grafana.

33
00:01:30.872 --> 00:01:35.726
Nikola: We can follow the service calls and database work without changing those

34
00:01:35.726 --> 00:01:36.130
Nikola: applications.

35
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.557
Tyler: Now look inside Checkout.

36
00:01:37.557 --> 00:01:39.770
Tyler: Where does most of the customer's wait go?

37
00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.358
Nikola: This failed connection takes about two seconds.

38
00:01:42.358 --> 00:01:46.570
Nikola: The order still completes, but the customer waits for a failed attempt first.

39
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.454
Tyler: Why is Checkout making that call?

40
00:01:48.454 --> 00:01:50.279
Tyler: OBI has shown us where to look.

41
00:01:50.279 --> 00:01:54.189
Tyler: Let's add the Java agent to connect the failed call to the work inside the

42
00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:54.450
Tyler: service.

43
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.090
Tyler: Here's the direct setup.

44
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:02.370
Tyler: Supply the Java agent, tell Java to load it, set the service name and choose the

45
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:02.700
Tyler: exporter.

46
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.670
Tyler: We also configure a span around the pricing method.

47
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.290
Tyler: The agent supplies the HTTP and database detail; it doesn't invent a span for

48
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:10.950
Tyler: every method.

49
00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.373
Nikola: We know this service and have access to its deployment.

50
00:02:14.373 --> 00:02:17.786
Nikola: Even then, somebody has to arrange the agent and startup settings.

51
00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:21.510
Nikola: Java loads the agent when it starts, so this requires a rollout.

52
00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.440
Tyler: Let's apply the prepared patch.

53
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.304
Tyler: The payload image is already available; this step changes the Deployment, not

54
00:02:27.304 --> 00:02:28.270
Tyler: the application image.

55
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.657
Nikola: Now we send fresh traffic.

56
00:02:35.657 --> 00:02:37.849
Nikola: Adding instrumentation hasn't fixed the delay.

57
00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:39.310
Nikola: It should explain it.

58
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.910
Tyler: The new trace has PricingEngine.calculate, the method we configured.

59
00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:47.943
Tyler: Inside it: the failed HTTP call, then the successful product-price query.

60
00:02:47.943 --> 00:02:51.610
Tyler: We can now name the operation and see its fallback.

61
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.593
Nikola: The span says SocketTimeoutException.

62
00:02:53.593 --> 00:02:56.502
Nikola: Its exception event says Connect timed out.

63
00:02:56.502 --> 00:03:01.490
Nikola: Two seconds trying the wrong address, then the fallback gets the price.

64
00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:02.837
Tyler: So the order works.

65
00:03:02.837 --> 00:03:05.917
Tyler: We just make the customer wait for a backend that isn't answering.

66
00:03:05.917 --> 00:03:07.970
Tyler: Let's look at the setting behind that address.

67
00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.027
Tyler: We expose this deployment setting as a resource attribute: LEGACY_PRICING_URL.

68
00:03:13.027 --> 00:03:15.270
Tyler: Its value matches the failed call.

69
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:20.129
Tyler: We know this backend was retired; this instance still tries it before the

70
00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:21.250
Tyler: working product store.

71
00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:25.516
Nikola: Now we can help the owning team with more than a slow-service alert.

72
00:03:25.516 --> 00:03:29.462
Nikola: Customers are losing two seconds here; here's the trace and a configuration PR

73
00:03:29.462 --> 00:03:30.980
Nikola: that removes the old setting.

74
00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.230
Nikola: We can bring them a fix based on what the application is actually doing.

75
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.103
Tyler: Let's remove the setting and roll Checkout.

76
00:03:38.103 --> 00:03:41.021
Tyler: Same application image, same Java agent, same exporter.

77
00:03:41.021 --> 00:03:41.750
Tyler: No rebuild.

78
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:48.764
Nikola: The rollout is ready.

79
00:03:48.764 --> 00:03:52.710
Nikola: Back to the customer: let's send a new order and open its trace.

80
00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.144
Tyler: Here's the new waterfall.

81
00:03:55.144 --> 00:03:56.845
Tyler: The failed legacy call is gone.

82
00:03:56.845 --> 00:04:00.247
Tyler: The product-price query is still there, and the order continues through the

83
00:04:00.247 --> 00:04:00.530
Tyler: application.

84
00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:01.555
Nikola: That's better.

85
00:04:01.555 --> 00:04:06.138
Nikola: The customer gets their order without waiting for a backend we've already turned

86
00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:06.490
Nikola: off.

87
00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.268
Tyler: We didn't make the timeout less visible.

88
00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:11.023
Tyler: We removed the unnecessary call.

89
00:04:11.023 --> 00:04:13.130
Tyler: Let's compare the request-duration metrics too.

90
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.703
Tyler: These captured Grafana windows show the average checkout dropping from about

91
00:04:18.703 --> 00:04:21.797
Tyler: two-point-two seconds to a hundred and sixty-five milliseconds.

92
00:04:21.797 --> 00:04:26.050
Tyler: They're a repeated-request comparison, alongside the fresh trace we just opened.

93
00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.690
Nikola: A customer gets their two seconds back.

94
00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:29.021
Nikola: Good.

95
00:04:29.021 --> 00:04:31.010
Nikola: Now what about the other teams?

96
00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:38.154
Nikola: We arranged the Java setup for Checkout because we knew the service and had

97
00:04:38.154 --> 00:04:38.570
Nikola: access.

98
00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.482
Nikola: Thirteen other services can use language agents.

99
00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:42.730
Nikola: Who's arranging those?

100
00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.144
Tyler: I'd rather not schedule thirteen versions of that meeting.

101
00:04:46.144 --> 00:04:47.862
Tyler: We'll use the OpenTelemetry Injector.

102
00:04:47.862 --> 00:04:51.644
Tyler: First, let's look at what the established Kubernetes Operator workflow still

103
00:04:51.644 --> 00:04:54.050
Tyler: needs from the platform and application teams.

104
00:04:55.370 --> 00:05:00.494
Tyler: The upstream OpenTelemetry Operator can inject agents, with opt-in on a workload

105
00:05:00.494 --> 00:05:01.775
Tyler: or a namespace.

106
00:05:01.775 --> 00:05:04.765
Tyler: But the platform still needs application facts.

107
00:05:04.765 --> 00:05:10.316
Tyler: This Python image uses musl; its Python version and native packages must match

108
00:05:10.316 --> 00:05:11.170
Tyler: the payload.

109
00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:14.707
Nikola: Which image, which runtime, who has the Dockerfile?

110
00:05:14.707 --> 00:05:16.717
Nikola: The application team usually knows.

111
00:05:16.717 --> 00:05:19.130
Nikola: The annotation doesn't answer those questions.

112
00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:24.023
Tyler: Add a dot net worker and we also need per-container language mappings and its

113
00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:24.677
Tyler: runtime settings.

114
00:05:24.677 --> 00:05:28.597
Tyler: If a container starts multiple languages, or an entrypoint ignores the injected

115
00:05:28.597 --> 00:05:31.210
Tyler: environment, more YAML alone won't make that work.

116
00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:36.170
Nikola: That's where a small deployment change can turn into hours of investigation and

117
00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.668
Nikola: days waiting for an owner or release.

118
00:05:38.668 --> 00:05:40.453
Nikola: The Operator helps with injection.

119
00:05:40.453 --> 00:05:45.450
Nikola: We'd like to prepare more of the routine runtime setup once, and share it.

120
00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.927
Tyler: The Injector is the shared library in this diagram.

121
00:05:49.927 --> 00:05:54.486
Tyler: It runs inside a compatible process at startup and loads the matching language

122
00:05:54.486 --> 00:05:54.837
Tyler: agent.

123
00:05:54.837 --> 00:05:59.046
Tyler: We prepare the library and supported agents together, instead of having every

124
00:05:59.046 --> 00:06:01.150
Tyler: service team assemble a language-specific installation.

125
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.809
Nikola: It works at process startup; it doesn't manage Kubernetes workloads.

126
00:06:04.809 --> 00:06:09.150
Nikola: We still need a controller to select the workloads and arrange the rollout.

127
00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:15.104
Tyler: For this talk, that controller is RIO: the Runtime Instrumentation Operator in

128
00:06:15.104 --> 00:06:15.491
Tyler: OTelX.

129
00:06:15.491 --> 00:06:20.533
Tyler: This demo uses the bundled prototype of that workflow, building on Jack Berg's

130
00:06:20.533 --> 00:06:20.921
Tyler: work.

131
00:06:20.921 --> 00:06:25.187
Tyler: RIO selects Kubernetes workloads and handles rollout; the Injector loads agents

132
00:06:25.187 --> 00:06:26.350
Tyler: inside the processes.

133
00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:31.744
Nikola: The idea we'd like to bring upstream is less routine runtime configuration for

134
00:06:31.744 --> 00:06:32.525
Nikola: each team.

135
00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:36.428
Nikola: RIO demonstrates it; the eventual upstream home is still open.

136
00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:39.550
Nikola: Compatibility and unusual startup behavior still need owners.

137
00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.346
Tyler: Let's install the controller.

138
00:06:41.346 --> 00:06:44.297
Tyler: We've prepared the agent bundle, exporter and exclusions.

139
00:06:44.297 --> 00:06:46.510
Tyler: No application namespace is enrolled yet.

140
00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:53.966
Nikola: Instrument demo-apps.

141
00:06:53.966 --> 00:06:56.054
Nikola: That's the selection: one namespace.

142
00:06:56.054 --> 00:07:01.068
Nikola: We haven't listed thirteen services or assigned a language to each one.

143
00:07:01.068 --> 00:07:04.410
Nikola: The prepared bundle supplies the supported runtime choices.

144
00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:05.443
Tyler: Apply it.

145
00:07:05.443 --> 00:07:08.650
Tyler: RIO performs the rolling restarts for the selected workloads.

146
00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:13.283
Tyler: Checkout keeps its existing direct agent, and the four Go services keep OBI.

147
00:07:13.283 --> 00:07:16.490
Tyler: Compatible new workloads in this namespace inherit the policy.

148
00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.390
Tyler: Fourteen services now have language agents.

149
00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:22.370
Tyler: The four Go services retain OBI.

150
00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:25.010
Tyler: Let's open the result in a fresh order.

151
00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:27.936
Nikola: Open Cart in this fresh request.

152
00:07:27.936 --> 00:07:32.280
Nikola: These are Ruby-agent observations; we didn't arrange a separate Ruby

153
00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.017
Nikola: installation for its team.

154
00:07:34.017 --> 00:07:37.926
Nikola: Now open Currency: that Go service still uses OBI.

155
00:07:37.926 --> 00:07:42.270
Nikola: Fourteen language agents, including Checkout, and four services with OBI.

156
00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:46.590
Tyler: As the SDKs start exporting, OBI recognizes them and steps back from tracing

157
00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:47.205
Tyler: those processes.

158
00:07:47.205 --> 00:07:50.590
Tyler: We leave it running for the services that still need it.

159
00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.970
Nikola: So the next team can start with its question.

160
00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.670
Nikola: Useful detail is already there.

161
00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.390
Nikola: That is the result of the namespace rollout.

162
00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:03.806
Tyler: An agent update next month shouldn't mean another setup project for thirteen

163
00:08:03.806 --> 00:08:04.149
Tyler: teams.

164
00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:08.609
Tyler: The platform can update the shared bundle and use the same rollout path.

165
00:08:08.609 --> 00:08:11.010
Tyler: Eligible new services inherit the namespace policy.

166
00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.807
Nikola: Compatibility tests, exceptions and recovery remain real work.

167
00:08:14.807 --> 00:08:17.850
Nikola: We centralize ownership of that shared path.

168
00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:22.631
Nikola: The saving is avoiding the routine setup and updates being arranged

169
00:08:22.631 --> 00:08:24.370
Nikola: independently over and over.

170
00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.468
Nikola: This is a sketch of language-agent adoption across eligible services.

171
00:08:29.468 --> 00:08:33.623
Nikola: With team-by-team work, one team finishes; another has a release freeze.

172
00:08:33.623 --> 00:08:39.290
Nikola: Add a service or rewrite one in another language, and the gap can open again.

173
00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:43.545
Tyler: The centralized path covers the eligible services in one rollout, before that

174
00:08:43.545 --> 00:08:44.528
Tyler: first team-by-team increment.

175
00:08:44.528 --> 00:08:46.168
Tyler: New compatible services inherit it.

176
00:08:46.168 --> 00:08:50.431
Tyler: We still maintain the shared path, but routine enrollment doesn't wait for a

177
00:08:50.431 --> 00:08:52.070
Tyler: separate project in every team.

178
00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.935
Tyler: Here's an example for this same eighteen-service application: the thirteen

179
00:08:56.935 --> 00:09:00.144
Tyler: remaining agent setups and two years of monthly updates.

180
00:09:00.144 --> 00:09:02.639
Tyler: Each step is another release to handle.

181
00:09:02.639 --> 00:09:06.917
Tyler: Distributed owners repeat that work; the centralized path shares the setup and

182
00:09:06.917 --> 00:09:07.630
Tyler: update rollouts.

183
00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.270
Nikola: The modeled difference is two hundred and forty-seven engineering hours.

184
00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:15.254
Nikola: At a hundred dollars an hour, that's twenty-four thousand seven hundred dollars

185
00:09:15.254 --> 00:09:15.918
Nikola: of capacity.

186
00:09:15.918 --> 00:09:17.910
Nikola: Time for the next customer problem.

187
00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:22.000
Tyler: These are assumed setup and rollout hours, not measured cash savings or a

188
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:22.870
Tyler: complete platform budget.

189
00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.770
Tyler: Put your own support and testing costs into the worksheet.

190
00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.670
Tyler: We haven't priced the calendar time waiting for other teams.

191
00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.755
Tyler: We started with a customer waiting for an order.

192
00:09:32.755 --> 00:09:34.303
Tyler: OBI showed the failed connection.

193
00:09:34.303 --> 00:09:36.159
Tyler: Java detail named the pricing operation.

194
00:09:36.159 --> 00:09:41.111
Tyler: We found the old endpoint setting, removed it, and opened a new trace to see the

195
00:09:41.111 --> 00:09:41.730
Tyler: wait disappear.

196
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:46.368
Nikola: Then one namespace choice added language detail across the supported fleet.

197
00:09:46.368 --> 00:09:50.685
Nikola: The next team shouldn't have to repeat our setup before investigating.

198
00:09:50.685 --> 00:09:54.610
Nikola: RIO demonstrates the shared workflow we'd like to bring upstream.

199
00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.406
Tyler: OpenTelemetry has grown beyond APIs and SDKs.

200
00:09:57.406 --> 00:10:02.359
Tyler: It's instrumentation you can install on a service or across a cluster, then use

201
00:10:02.359 --> 00:10:06.250
Tyler: to find what's keeping a customer waiting—and do something about it.

202
00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:07.576
Nikola: Find the wait.

203
00:10:07.576 --> 00:10:08.582
Nikola: Fix the cause.

204
00:10:08.582 --> 00:10:10.259
Nikola: Make the next investigation easier.

205
00:10:10.259 --> 00:10:10.930
Nikola: Thank you.
